{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/284798-man-amplifiers","id":284798,"title":"Man Amplifiers","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum","Trinity Continuum: \u00c6on"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-10-17T03:07:32-07:00","id":284798,"title":"Man Amplifiers","content":"I've been GMing a game of Aeon Trinity (under the Storyteller system) for a few months now, and I have noticed that powered armour (or armor if you don't use UK English) isn't really an element of the setting. Besides the once off freak of nature that is the bioVAS. So that got me to wonder why powered armour took such a backseat in this setting? I can understand for the purposes of game that its about the Psions and not about the Tech (thankyou FAQ website), though I have found my group of psions are squishy compared to just a single abby. For those who know the abberant Anders Nash from the Darkness Revealed Adventures he is basically the reason my group has a pathological fear of fighting aberrants. If he is an example of a run of the mill abberant what chance if hell does a group of diversified Psions have in killing him? I even toned down his healing factor because he almost TPKed the group on his own with just his powers.<br /> <br /> This brings me to the subject of armours. Alot of the reason the team were so squishy is that the standard armours in the core book are incredibly lackluster. Sure the team is good against normal people and other psions but against aberrants they might as well nuke the site from orbit and move on with their lives which is infact who they killed Nash in the end, by blowing up the Freya with him on board. Now it can be argued that Psions are meant to go toe to toe with aberrants but then there is a major conceit of the Trinity setting proven wrong right there. That Psions (not plural) can stand up to an abby.<br /> <br /> Though I am still willing to believe that the groups inexperience may be a major contributing factor. So my <i>real</i> reason for this topic is to hear thoughts on the subject of powered armour and defences in this setting and what older more experienced GMs them myself did.<br /> <br /> Note that this is intended to be a discussion on two fronts, effective aberrant counter measures and power armour, not about VARGS or vehicles brought to bear against abbies. It goes rather against the mayor conceit of the setting if psions can't fight your average aberrant even when that abby is outnumbred four too one.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Man's unfailing capacity to believe what he prefers to be true rather than what the evidence shows to be likely and possible has always astounded me. We long for a caring Universe which will save us from our childish mistakes, and in the face of mountains of evidence to the contrary we will pin all our hopes on the slimmest of doubts. God has not been proven not to exist, therefore he must exist.</i> <b>--Academician Prokhor Zakharov</b>","edited":{"userid":3246,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3246-galaxy-master-zhad","user":"Galaxy Master Zhad","date":". 10-17-2014, 06:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3246-galaxy-master-zhad","name":"Galaxy Master Zhad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3246_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-17T09:29:23-07:00","id":285018,"title":"","content":"I saw 'Man Amplifiers' and really expected to see a very different type of post <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> My take. And spoilers for those that haven't played the DR books or read the old metaplot.<br /> <br /> The abbie to Psion ratio of it taking a well co-ordinated party of Psions to put down one low-mid level aberrant has always been a key part of the appeal for me. Aberrants are more powerful, have been around longer and should be bloody dangerous - psions are weaker, their powers are artificially restricted (see the Chambers / meta plot). Psions need BFG's plus psi powers, rapid thinking and teamwork to stay alive.<br /> <br /> Psions are not weak compared to neutrals of course, but they are not as potent in terms of raw power as Aberrants.<br /> <br /> I've run the same encounter you mentioned a few times now (love the DR stuff), and in every case that fight is a huge, huge deal. But, the players made it through by being inventive. If they go for a straight up toe-to-toe combat they're going to be very badly hurt - and they should be. Psions need to be smarter or they get killed. That combat is brutal, its truly desperate, its in close confines as the proverbial is about to hit the fan. The characters are scrambling to stay alive when its all going to hell. Characters need to come up with every trick they could think of and be well coordinated to put Anders Nash down or get out of his way. While challenging, this is also what makes that fight truly memorable.<br /> <br /> I had the approach that the party was by the end of the fight likely to be down to their last health levels, last few rounds of ammo, and barely any psi and / or willpower left. If they survived they would be exhausted - and yes some may even die. IIRC in the plot they then get to stay with the Chinese on Mars as they are 'processed' so a bit of downtime can take care of particularly bad injuries, psi pool and willpower issues.<br /> <br /> Even 10+ years after running that for the first time I still have players talk about what an absolute SOB Anders Nash was - but they do so with a sense of &quot;man we pulled that off - god knows how but we did, but we did!&quot;. At the time, of course, the reaction was &quot;<i>what the hell aggggh god gettitoffmeshootit!!!?!??!&quot;</i>, heh.<br /> <br /> Answering your other question regarding, ahem, man amplifiers, I'm not especially keen on seeing a single Psion (or even a couple of them) being able to put down an abbie. I've always taken it as Aberrants being the (Ridley Scott) 'Alien' of the Trinity setting (which makes Khantze Lu Ge absolutely terrifying).<br /> <br /> As a garden variety party of low to mid level psions, if you're up against an Aberrant you may loose people. You may barely come out of the fight alive. <i><b>It should be very hard.</b></i> Power armo(u)r reduces the threat. Ramp up the power of psions too much and you run the risk of making Aberrants, if not trivial, 'just another monster'. That cheapens them, to me.<br /> <br /> Aberrants are <i><b>the</b></i> elemental threat to humanity. They damn near destroyed the human race before, there's every chance they could do so in the future. Players should be terrified of their characters fighting Aberrants. Doesn't mean they get to avoid fighting them though, and it doesn't mean they'll survive when it goes down.<br /> <br /> Now, Aeon has tons of other (somewhat) less terrifying / powerful challenges for psions to deal with so the stakes aren't always so high (neuts, rogue psions, chromies, whack-job security officers, option-8, merc teams, crackpot governments and so on). Because of all that I've not felt the need to bring Aberrants into a game too often - and Aberrants are not, IMO, the real focus of the DR games in any event - they were set special pieces.<br /> <br /> I keep Aberrants as deadly, as something very special, something that should get every player in the group swearing loudly when they realize they're up against one. I wouldn't want to see power armo(u)r showing up except in very rare circumstances.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#ff0000\"><b>Ad Astra</b></span>","edited":{"userid":232,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","user":"CHILL","date":". 10-17-2014, 06:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","name":"CHILL","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar232_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"248"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-17T20:00:53-07:00","id":285642,"title":"","content":"CHILL has it right: Any individual full-on Aberrants are most likely going to turn any individual psion into paste. But bear in mind that the prevailing theme of the game is <i>Unity</i>. Psions are designed (both in and out of character) to require them to work together to bring down powerful Aberrants. While not as clearly one-sided, even weaker sub-Aberrant mutants should still be a challenge to the lone psion.<br /> <br /> Securing humanity's future means working together, both at the scale of psions and up at the scale of the psi orders and world governments. The Aeon Trinity works at both levels to help make that happen.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:#800000\">Ian A. A. Watson</span></b><span style=\"color:#bdc3c7\">(he/she)</span><br /> <b>Onyx Path Community Manager<br /> Trinity Continuum Content Lead</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4-ianwatson","name":"IanWatson","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4_2.png","title":"Community Manager","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1512"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-18T00:47:25-07:00","id":285767,"title":"","content":"This may be asking more than you can share at this time - but do Psion abilities stack in &quot;more than the sum of our parts&quot; ways better than Novas/Aberrants do?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-18T08:22:50-07:00","id":285935,"title":"","content":"There's been little snippets here and there over the Internet - all to be taken with a healthy pinch of salt. For example, there was a thread on RPG.net a while back that discussed in very very loose terms some expectations of how powers fit into the setting, relative scales and so on and I think something about teamwork came up briefly. There was nothing at a game mechanics level though (as its too early for that). We're maintaining a shared doc over in the Trinity Continuum fan community that pulls these snippets for the new games into one place. You can take a look at it over here (and add to it!): <a href=\"http://bit.ly/1g3AhR6\" target=\"_blank\">http://bit.ly/1g3AhR6</a><br /> <br /> Although most of that stuff comes from people involved at one point or another with the reboots - treat everything you see in that document as subject to change / whims of the developers. Makes for a fun read though and of course wild speculation doesn't hurt in the meantime until we get something official when Ian and Co are ready to unleash it on us <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#ff0000\"><b>Ad Astra</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","name":"CHILL","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar232_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"248"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-18T08:42:19-07:00","id":285949,"title":"","content":"Galaxy Master Zhad, Not sure if this is any use to you. <br /> <br /> One of the things I do as a GM when using pivotal scenes like the fight with Anders Nash is basically gloss over the rules and focus on tone and outcome. I use the stats presented to get a sense of what the writer of the scenario had in mind, then use that as inspiration for making it up as I go along in the scene - not that the players know I'm doing that. For individual major challenges like Anders Nash, it doesn't matter if I use the 'rules' - it matters how the players feel when they come out of it at the end. For that encounter (based on my opinion of the 'Alien movie' nature of how Aberrants should be presented), I wanted player's characters to come out of it dreading another round, and mortally terrified of Aberrants.<br /> <br /> It does mean I can't rely on games mechanics to 'decide' if a PC ends up dead or alive - that has to be my call as a result of that style of GMing. My approach is to avoid TPK's, but absolutely deal fairly harshly when a player goes all gung-ho or does something really stupid (I have little patience for 'crazy' players at the best of times). The few times I've run that particular encounter there's been no character deaths. It has been very, very close - and unless my players are reading this now they would have no clue that I fudged the whole thing. <br /> <br /> They just came away with 'Aberrant = brown trousers time'.<br /> <br /> Of course, you can't fudge it like that with more 'routine' bad guys as inconsistency starts to creep in and can drag a game down and creates a sense of no control for players, but for things like Anders Nash - hell yeah.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#ff0000\"><b>Ad Astra</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","name":"CHILL","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar232_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"248"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-20T16:21:41-07:00","id":288043,"title":"","content":"Hehe...man amplifiers. I swear I giggled for about 30 seconds straight when I first read that. Psions should treat even a sub abberant like the main characters in The Matrix treated agents. (The first one...the sequels well...that was more of a hundred xp psion with lots of biotech and psi out the wazoo fighting sub aberrants to me)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=392\" data-tempid=\"temp_392_1519878565794_106\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3676-manbearpig","name":"ManBearPig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3676_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-21T12:24:23-07:00","id":288760,"title":"","content":"I think Trinity didn't really quite have the chops to be a giant robot game, which is the direction the VARGs were headed in. You have a larger selection of them in the Trinity Technology manual, but I think when the early game stats were written no one was aware of just how powerful Aberrants would eventually become.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-22T07:30:20-07:00","id":289570,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wolfgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/284798-man-amplifiers?p=288760#post288760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I think Trinity didn't really quite have the chops to be a giant robot game, which is the direction the VARGs were headed in. You have a larger selection of them in the Trinity Technology manual, but I think when the early game stats were written no one was aware of just how powerful Aberrants would eventually become.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hrm...not sure about that for a couple of reasons. <br /> <br /> I got the sense though that Aberrants in Trinity were largely as intended. I don't think VARGs started to appear because of some issue with (Aeon/Trinity book) Aberrant power levels - Aberrants were meant to be a problem for psions, as I mentioned in an earlier post. Unless you're referring mechanics published in the Aberrant line and using Novas in the Trinity/Aeon era?<br /> <br /> I think powered armor VARGs have a use, but are not common for a bunch of reasons including training needed to use them, cost, technological complexity, being restricted military machines and so on. I still like the idea actually of allowing a game of VARG pilots going toe to toe with Abbies - and that can be done even with the old rules - but i'd run that as a game a very specific focus - &quot;VARG Top Gun&quot;, if you will. I wouldn't make VARGs an option for most Aeon games.<br /> <br /> Which brings me onto the tech manual. The VAS in the corebook got pretty much re-written as VARGS in the tech manual - and there were a <i><b>ton</b></i> of issues with that book. It felt to me like the tech manual didn't really fit well with the rest of the line and some of the mechanics / stats introduced in it were...... um, <i><b>very</b></i> funky. I recall hearing that some writers weren't overly happy with the end result too (from Bruce B maybe - can't recall where right now, rpg.net, maybe?).<br /> <br /> I'd be surprised if we didn't get a big book of nifty tech in some form or another for Aeon so seeing it done right could be interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#ff0000\"><b>Ad Astra</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","name":"CHILL","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar232_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"248"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-23T12:58:58-07:00","id":290733,"title":"","content":"The early game stats I mention were the ones from the tech manual, not from published Aberrants in Trinity (which I never saw stats for since I don't buy modules).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-07T09:37:27-08:00","id":333105,"title":"","content":"I had a few scenes involving a VARG pilot rival of a PC during Descent into Darkness, specifically during the biorg hunt - the same pilot appeared in a 2120's game with Tainted Nova PC's (we had evil parties in all our games at the time, running parrallel, good times) and despite the custom VARG chassis and dual plasma cannons, still proved to be a paper tiger against the aberrant system nova creations. VARGs occupy a place as a Sci-Fi staple and i'd hate to see them go (especially the Nihonjin hard-tech giants), but in toe-to-toe with aberrants they really aren't more than a potential tomb for a player to be melted into or in the best case a chance to buy a turn or two for the rest of the party before bailing out. If you're going toe-to-toe with a combat specialized Aberrant circa 2120, nothing less than a fully armed and manned battlecruiser and i believe the Tech manual gives a total number of these vessels in a single digit. Even then you are likely going to be sacrificing a few pawns if you want to survive.<br /> The others telling tales of Anders Nash aren't kidding, the encounter on the Freya is character defining stuff (and the looks on the PC's faces are worth the fudging) - Psions can't really 'fight' an aberrant in those isolated cramped conditions and must concentrate mostly on surviving it - it's a defining element of the series, but it is most pronounced in Trinity with some very old aberrants lurking about - sometimes closer than you were lead to beleive. Out of interest i had a machine emulated Anders Nash show up as a data backup later for the Aberrant party to take on - he was fun.<br /> To Anders Nash.<br /> -toasts-","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-12T06:04:28-08:00","id":338005,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I saw 'Man Amplifiers' and really expected to see a very different type of post <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hehe...man amplifiers. I swear I giggled for about 30 seconds straight when I first read that. </div> </div> </div>C'mon, you know every male Norca has tried it at least once. Hell, if they can get the bioware figured out then they're probably selling it on the black market. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1849-suddenthunder","name":"SuddenThunder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1849_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"563"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-12T17:49:28-08:00","id":338581,"title":"","content":"Good Lord, Ian. We're sitting on a working man amplifier and we're writing role-playing games? We could make a fortune!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Neall Raemonn Price</span><br /> Beleaguered Scion Developer</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/137-neall","name":"Neall","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar137_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1204"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-06T00:33:16-08:00","id":361062,"title":"","content":"Thanks for all the replies everyone! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I know this comes a few month's later, as myself and my gaming group took a break near December from Trinity for a bit - in the wind down we played a game of Aberrant, using an alternate rule system I have been wanting to try out for years - thus leading to my extended quiet here. In the meantime since my last post the party actually got through the last part of Darkness Revealed: Descent into Darkness and from that it would seem they have at last learned teamwork of some sort! I hope I one day understand why my IRL game group will always be the biggest, meanest, most paranoid bunch of people - usually to each other in RP games.<br /> <br /> I also took some time out to hypothesis the &quot;ranks&quot; of armours;<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:times new roman\">Ratings have been adjusted to reflect a broader range of armour types. <i>Basic</i> armour covers ratings 1 to 2, this reflects the protection provided by simple or crude defensive measure or means. These armours are typical of a civilian type and are the most common. <i>Intermediate</i> armour covers ratings 3 to 4, these are materials or mechanisms designed to take some punishment our to counter light arms. These armous are typically employed by private security or special task forces. <i>Advanced</i> armour covers ratings 5 to 6, these are typically full body suits usually employed by various armed forces or thick ablative layers or active defence countermeasures. Resistances of these levels are meant to deter light weapons fire as such armours of this rank is normally heavily restricted and used almost exclusively by the military. <i>Superior</i> armours cover rating 7 to 8 though theoretically 9 and 10 are available as well (however armour ratings of 9 to 10 should be an exception to an already exceptional situation), these ratings reflect the very, very best in personal defence and as such is not obtainable over regular channels. These are exotic materials or technologies that can only be acquired through the Storyteller's intervention and as such should be used sparingly.</span></span></div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><span style=\"font-family:times new roman, times, serif\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"> An armour may be modified or improved in some way to give it a higher rating, however under no condition can such improvement increase the armour's rating by more than 1. Armours of different ranks do not stack atop each other unless stated otherwise in the description.</span></span></span></div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:times new roman\"> An armour's Lethal soak will usually be higher then it's Bashing soak, this is because Lethal damage is a greater threat to the user's life while dealing with relatively minor blows is not the most important feature of armour designs. There are of course exceptions.</span></span></div> <br /> I also came up with some of my own armours in 3-6 range but have made sure that they are restricted and not commercially available. It is my intent to make sure the PCs don't get anything in the 5+ range for longer than small part in any story.<br /> <br /> I am embarrassed to admit I wanted to make my own rather absurd gun then realized that the thing would not only violate several laws but physics too (I don't truck with the psuedo-gravity grids, SPIN GRAVITY ALL THE WAY!) so I scrapped the &quot;faux-blackhole gun&quot; and the Trinity FAQ site did remind me that this game is about the PSIONS and their powers not the TECH. So that was also a heaping help of bringing me down to earth.<br /> <br /> I will be running a startup adventure again this Thursday mostly digging into a few interesting plot threads left over from Darkness Revealed (specifically how the Willoms managed to managed to get past the Martian Orbit Guard - yeah another thing I made up - and corruption in the Chinese Military/Government in and around Mars) and a B plot about a freighter lost between Mars and the Asteroid Belt. Lots of outlaw and pirate fun to be had out on the frontier.<br /> <br /> Thanks again for everyone's input.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Man's unfailing capacity to believe what he prefers to be true rather than what the evidence shows to be likely and possible has always astounded me. We long for a caring Universe which will save us from our childish mistakes, and in the face of mountains of evidence to the contrary we will pin all our hopes on the slimmest of doubts. God has not been proven not to exist, therefore he must exist.</i> <b>--Academician Prokhor Zakharov</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3246-galaxy-master-zhad","name":"Galaxy Master Zhad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3246_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}